    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To simplify $(1 + \\tan 20^\\circ)(1 + \\tan 25^\\circ)$, we can use the tangent addition formula, which states that:\n\n$$\\tan(A + B) = \\frac{\\tan A + \\tan B}{1 - \\tan A \\cdot \\tan B}$$\n\nLet $A = 20^\\circ$ and $B = 25^\\circ$. Then:\n\n$$\\tan(A + B) = \\tan(20^\\circ + 25^\\circ) = \\frac{\\tan(20^\\circ) + \\tan(25^\\circ)}{1 - \\tan(20^\\circ) \\cdot \\tan(25^\\circ)}$$\n\nWe know that $\\tan(90^\\circ) \\neq 1$, so we can take the reciprocal of each term and then multiply:\n\n$$\\frac{1}{\\tan(20^\\circ) + \\tan(25^\\circ)} = \\frac{(1 - \\tan(20^\\circ) \\cdot \\tan(25^\\circ))}{\\tan(90^\\circ) - 1 + \\tan^2(20^\\circ) \\cdot \\tan^2(25^\\circ)}$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $\\tan(20^\\circ) + \\tan(25^\\circ)$, we get:\n\n$$(1 + \\tan(20^\\circ))(1 + \\tan(25^\\circ)) \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\tan(20^\\circ) + \\tan(25^\\circ)} = \\frac{\\tan^2(20^\\circ) \\cdot \\tan^2(25^\\circ)}{\\tan(90^\\circ) - 1}$$\n\nNow, we can simplify the right side of the equation:\n\n$$\\frac{\\tan^2(20^\\circ) \\cdot \\tan^2(25^\\circ)}{\\tan(90^\\circ) - 1} = \\frac{\\tan(40^\\circ) \\cdot \\tan(55^\\circ)}{\\tan(45^\\circ)}$$\n\nSince $\\tan(40^\\circ) \\cdot \\tan(55^\\circ) = \\tan(90^\\circ)$ and $\\tan(45^\\circ) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}$, we can simplify further:\n\n$$\\frac{\\tan(40^\\circ) \\cdot \\tan(55^\\circ)}{\\tan(45^\\circ)} = \\boxed{2}$$",
